<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French West India Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1664">1664</a>–<a href="/years/1674">1674</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Compagnie des Indes occidentales</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Company was a chartered company founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> in 1664 to run <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s colonies and trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. It took over the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands, including Guadeloupe, Grenada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a>, and in 1665 appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-dogeron">Bertrand d'Ogeron</a> governor of Tortuga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. It failed financially and was dissolved in 1674, when the colonies passed to the crown.</p></div>
